[Event "Rook endgames: behind the passed pawn"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "teaching"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/vZQa2yT6"]
[FEN "8/8/1k1P4/1r6/R5K1/8/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/1k1P4/1r6/R5K1/8/8/8_w_-_-_0_1"]

{ Time to learn rook endings. First rule: your rook belongs behind the passed pawn. [%cal Ya4d4] }
1. Rd4 { Behind the pawn, where it belongs. Now nothing stops it. [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd6d8] } (1. d7 { The pawn runs off alone. Watch him park in front of it. } 1... Rd5 2. d8=Q+ Rxd8 { Your new queen lasted one move. Drawn. }) (1. Ra6+ { A free check, and a free rook for him. } 1... Kxa6 { The rook is gone and the pawn falls next. Lost. }) (1. Rb4 { You offer a trade and he is delighted. } 1... Rxb4+ { Now the pawn is all alone. You are lost. }) (1. Ra5 { Sideways, and your rook lands right beside his. } 1... Rxa5 { The rook is gone. You are lost. }) (1. Ra8 { Far from the pawn, and far from safe. } 1... Rb4+ 2. Kf3 Rb3+ { The checks never stop. Drawn. }) 1... Kc5 { He comes for your rook. Push the pawn anyway. [%cal Yd6d7] } 2. d7 { The rook shields the pawn from behind. Now watch the pawn go home. [%csl Gd7][%tail] } (2. Rd1 { Also winning. Still behind the pawn, just further back. } 2... Kc6 { The pawn goes home all the same. }) (2. Rd2 { The same idea, one square along. Also winning. } 2... Kc6 { Behind the pawn is behind the pawn. Still winning. }) (2. Rd3 { Also fine. Any square behind the pawn does the job. } 2... Kc6 { The pawn walks home from here. }) (2. Rd5+ { A check, but you have given the rook away. } 2... Kxd5 3. d7 Rb8 { His rook stops the pawn. You are lost. }) (2. Rc4+ { Another check that hangs your rook. } 2... Kxc4 3. Kf3 Rd5 { The pawn falls next. You are lost. }) (2. Ra4 { The rook leaves the pawn to fend for itself. } 2... Kxd6 { And the pawn is simply eaten. Drawn. }) 2... Kxd4 3. d8=Q+ { A new queen on d8, with check. A queen against a rook, and that is a win. [%csl Gd8] } 1-0


[Event "Rook endgames: get behind his runner"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[IntendedResult "draw"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/5haHjaqz"]
[FEN "4k3/8/3K4/8/4P3/8/7r/R7 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/4k3/8/3K4/8/4P3/8/7r/R7_w_-_-_0_1"]

{ He has the extra pawn and his king already sits on d6, ready to escort it home. A draw is a fine result here, so can you hold? }
1. e5 { The pawn sets off. Which square kills it dead? } 1... Re2 { Right behind it, and it cannot take another step. [%csl Ge2][%cal Ge2e5] } (1... Rd2+ { A check that only helps his king forward. } 2. Ke6 Kd8 3. Ra8+ { You are driven back and the pawn walks in. Lost. }) (1... Rh6+ { The same trouble: the check sends him where he wants to go. } 2. e6 Kf8 3. Ra8+ { You are lost. His rook pins yours to the back row while the pawn queens. }) (1... Kf7 { You step aside and the pawn is free. } 2. e6+ Kf6 3. Rf1+ { Lost. The pawn runs while your king chases checks instead of the runner. }) (1... Ra2 { You offer a trade you cannot afford. } 2. Rxa2 { A bare king against a pawn. Lost. }) (1... Rh1 { Wandering off, and the rook is not even safe there. } 2. Rxh1 { Lost. Your rook is gone for nothing, and the pawn walks in behind it. }) 2. e6 { He pushes past your rook. Now what? } 2... Rd2+ { Check from the d column, and his king must leave d6. Your king on e8 is already sitting in the pawn's road: now watch him try. [%csl Gd2][%cal Ge8e6][%tail] } (2... Rxe6+ { A free pawn! But look at what is left. } 3. Kxe6 { A bare king against a rook. You are lost. }) (2... Re1 { Behind the pawn again, but he was quicker. } 3. Ra8# { Mate. Your king is boxed in on the back row with no square left to run to. }) (2... Kf8 { The king wanders off the pawn's file. } 3. Ra8+ { And the pawn queens behind you. Lost. }) 3. Ke5 3... Ke7 4. Ra7+ 4... Ke8 5. e7 5... Rd7 { You offer the trade on d7. With the rooks gone, nobody walks that pawn home. } 6. Rxd7 6... Kxd7 7. e8=Q+ 7... Kxe8 { A new queen on e8, and your king ate her at once. Two bare kings on an empty board, and that is a draw. [%csl Ge8] } 0-1


[Event "Rook endgames: your rook is attacked"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/Gi4bWJcb"]
[FEN "8/4k3/8/8/1r6/K3p3/6R1/8 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/4k3/8/8/1r6/K3p3/6R1/8_b_-_-_0_1"]

{ His king is attacking your rook, and your own pawn needs a friend. One square does both. }
1... Re4 { Behind your own pawn, and safe. Now the pawn can never be blocked. [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge3e1] } (1... e2 { The pawn is in a hurry and runs straight into a rook. } 2. Rxe2+ Kd7 3. Kxb4 { Your pawn went, and now your rook too. Lost. }) (1... Ra4+ { A check with a hanging rook. } 2. Kxa4 { The rook is gone and the game with it. Lost. }) (1... Rb3+ { The same idea, the same problem. } 2. Kxb3 { Lost. His king simply captures the checking rook. }) (1... Rb2 { Guarding the pawn from the side, right next to his king. } 2. Kxb2 { Lost. The rook stood beside his king instead of behind your pawn, and now it is simply gone. }) (1... Kd6 { The king comes, but the rook was hanging. } 2. Kxb4 { Lost. His king scoops up the rook you left unguarded. }) 2. Re2 { He blocks in front of the pawn. It cannot move, so who can? } 2... Kd6 { The king marches over to help. Nothing else can. [%csl Gd6] } (2... Ke6 { Also good. Any step towards the pawn does the job. } 3. Kb2 Ke5 { Still winning. The king arrives just the same. }) (2... Kf6 { Also fine, and only one square slower. } 3. Kb2 Ke5 { Still winning. Your king arrives at the pawn just as well from here. }) 3. Kb2 { His king runs back to help. Keep coming. } 3... Ke5 { Straight at the pawn. Your king gets there first. [%csl Ge5][%tail] } (3... Kd5 { Also towards the pawn, but one row too low. } 4. Kc3 { His king slips in front and the win is gone. }) (3... Rd4 { The rook wanders off and leaves your pawn alone. } 4. Rxe3 { It is simply eaten. Drawn. }) 4. Kc1 4... Kf4 5. Re1 5... Kf3 6. Rh1 6... Rc4+ 7. Kd1 7... e2+ 8. Kd2 8... Rd4+ 9. Kc2 9... Kg2 10. Rh4 10... Rxh4 { You take it. His king stands alone against a king, a rook, and a pawn one step from queening. } 0-1


[Event "Rook endgames: build the wall"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "teaching"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/fdGo4RY7"]
[FEN "8/k7/8/3P4/r7/3K4/6R1/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/k7/8/3P4/r7/3K4/6R1/8_w_-_-_0_1"]

{ A new word: cutting off. Build a wall with your rook and his king can never cross it. [%cal Yg2b2] }
1. Rb2 { The wall is up on the b column. His king is locked on the wrong side. [%csl Gb2][%cal Gb2b8] } (1. d6 { The pawn is in a hurry, and now the checks begin. } 1... Ra3+ 2. Ke2 Ra2+ { They never stop. Drawn. }) (1. Rg7+ { A check that chases him nowhere useful. } 1... Ka6 2. d6 Ra1 { He gets behind the pawn and holds. Drawn. }) (1. Ra2 { A trade offer he is happy to take. } 1... Rxa2 { His rook is left and yours is not. You are lost. }) (1. Rg4 { The rook steps into the open. } 1... Rxg4 { You are lost. His rook takes yours for nothing. }) (1. Kc3 { You walk the king up, but the checks come first. } 1... Ra3+ 2. Kb2 Rd3 { He gets in front of the pawn. Drawn. }) 1... Rf4 { He shuffles about behind the wall. Now walk your king up. [%cal Yd3e3] } 2. Ke3 { The king climbs, the wall holds, and the pawn follows. [%csl Ge3][%tail] } (2. Kc3 { Also winning. The king can climb on either side. } 2... Rf3+ 3. Kc4 Rf4+ { Still winning: the checks slow you down but never catch your king. }) (2. Rb1 { Also fine. The wall is a column, not a square. } 2... Rf3+ { The checks come, but the wall still holds. }) (2. Rb5 { Also winning. The wall can slide up the column. } 2... Ka6 { Still winning. The wall holds no matter which square on the column it sits on. }) (2. d6 { Too soon. The pawn needs the king, not a head start. } 2... Rf6 3. d7 Rd6+ { He wins the pawn. Drawn. }) (2. Rb7+ { A check that gives the rook away. } 2... Kxb7 { You are lost. His king simply captures the checking rook. }) (2. Ra2+ { You give up the wall for one check. } 2... Kb6 3. d6 Rf1 { He is back in the game. Drawn. }) 2... Rc4 3. d6 3... Ka6 4. d7 4... Rc7 5. d8=Q { A new queen on d8. The wall never let his king or his rook anywhere near the action. } 1-0


[Event "Rook endgames: a wall on the fifth rank"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "review"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/2jeUUw1L"]
[FEN "4r3/1R6/8/4P3/8/8/k5K1/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/4r3/1R6/8/4P3/8/8/k5K1/8_w_-_-_0_1"]

{ His king is miles away in the corner. Build a wall and keep him there. }
1. Rb5 { A wall along the fifth row. His king can never get above it. [%csl Gb5][%cal Ga5h5] } (1. e6 { The pawn runs and his rook is waiting. } 1... Rxe6 { The pawn is gone. Drawn. It ran off with no king to guard it. }) (1. Ra7+ { A check, and then the pawn falls anyway. } 1... Kb3 2. e6 Rxe6 { Drawn. The check bought no time, and his rook simply eats the runner. }) (1. Kf3 { You bring the king, but the pawn was hanging. } 1... Rxe5 { Drawn. Undefended, the pawn falls in one move. }) (1. Rb2+ { The rook steps next to his king. } 1... Kxb2 { You are lost. His king simply captures the rook that checked it. }) (1. Rb4 { One row short, and the wall leaks. } 1... Rxe5 { The pawn is gone. Drawn. His rook slips through the gap and takes it. }) 1... Ka3 { He wanders near his own corner, going nowhere. } 2. Kf3 { Your king sets off, and the wall does the guarding. [%csl Gf3] } (2. Kg3 { Also winning. Any road towards the pawn is fine. } 2... Ka4 3. Rd5 { Still winning. The wall just slides to a new square on the same row, and it still reaches from a5 to h5. [%cal Ga5h5] }) (2. Rd5 { Also good, and you can do this later. } 2... Ka4 3. Kf3 { Still winning. Your king strolls up while the wall guards the pawn by itself. }) (2. Kf2 { Also winning. Any road towards the pawn will do. } 2... Ka4 { Still winning. Any path your king takes gets there just the same. }) (2. e6 { The pawn cannot go alone. } 2... Rxe6 { Drawn. It had no escort, and his rook simply eats it. }) (2. Ra5+ { A check that throws away the wall. } 2... Kb4 3. Ra1 Rxe5 { Drawn. Chasing the check let go of the wall, and the pawn falls. }) (2. Rb3+ { The rook is one step from his king. } 2... Kxb3 { You are lost. The check landed right next to his king, and it just takes the rook. }) 2... Ka4 { He attacks your rook. Save it, but keep the wall. } 3. Rd5 { The rook is safe, the wall holds, and now it guards the pawn. [%csl Gd5][%tail] } (3. Rc5 { Also good. The wall just has to stay on the fifth row. } 3... Ka3 { Still winning. His king wanders while the wall keeps doing its job. }) (3. e6 { You push, and forget your rook was attacked. } 3... Kxb5 { The rook is gone. You are lost. His king simply eats the rook you left hanging. }) (3. Rb4+ { You hand over the rook for a check. } 3... Kxb4 { Lost. You traded the rook for one check, and nothing is left to escort the pawn. }) 3... Kb3 4. Ke4 4... Rxe5+ 5. Rxe5 { You take back. His rook is gone, and a bare king can never trouble a king and rook. } 1-0


[Event "Rook endgames: the defender builds one too"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[IntendedResult "draw"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/QrceVMS9"]
[FEN "4R3/8/8/rp6/4P3/2K5/8/1k6 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/4R3/8/8/rp6/4P3/2K5/8/1k6_b_-_-_0_1"]

{ His pawn on e4 wants to run and his king on c3 is heading for your pawn on b5. Your job is to survive: one move stops both. }
1... Ra4 { A wall on the fourth row. It blocks the pawn and the king at once. [%csl Ga4][%cal Ga4h4] } (1... b4+ { Pushing with check feels great, and it loses. } 2. Kxb4 Ra7 3. Kc5 { Your pawn is gone and his runs. Lost. }) (1... Ra3+ { A check first, and the wall never gets built. } 2. Kb4 Ra7 3. e5 { His pawn is away. Lost. }) (1... Ra2 { Too low. The pawn simply walks past. } 2. e5 Ra4 3. Rb8 { Lost. His rook slips behind your own pawn and stops it cold. }) (1... Ra8 { You offer the trade, and the pawn ending is lost. } 2. Rxa8 b4+ 3. Kb3 { Lost. Without rooks, his extra pawn just walks home. }) (1... Kc1 { The king steps out and forgets the pawn. } 2. e5 Ra4 3. Rb8 { Lost. His rook gets behind your pawn, and the king never came to help. }) 2. e5 { The pawn walks up to your wall. Now hold it. } 2... Re4 { You slide along the wall and stand right in front of him. [%csl Ge4] } (2... b4+ { The check again, and again it costs everything. } 3. Kb3 Ra2 4. e6 { His pawn queens first. Lost. }) (2... Rc4+ { A check, but you gave up the wall. } 3. Kd3 Rc1 4. Rb8 { Lost. His rook gets behind your pawn now that yours has left the row. }) (2... Rb4 { Guarding your own pawn instead. } 3. Kxb4 { The rook is gone. Lost. }) (2... Ra3+ { Off the fourth row, and his pawn is free. } 3. Kb4 Re3 4. Ka5 { Lost. His king steps clear of the checks, and the pawn queens next. }) 3. e6 { He pushes past you. Now your own pawn saves you. } 3... b4+ { Now the check works: it drives his king away from everything. [%csl Gb4][%tail] } (3... Rxe6 { The free pawn, and his rook takes yours. } 4. Rxe6 { Your rook is gone and the pawn queens. Lost. }) (3... Re5 { You block in front of the pawn instead of checking. } 4. Kd4 { His king attacks your rook and the pawn goes through. Lost. }) (3... Rc4+ { The wrong check. His king simply steps up. } 4. Kd3 { Lost. His king shelters right next to the pawn, and the check achieved nothing. }) 4. Kd2 4... Rc4 5. e7 5... Rc7 6. Kd3 6... b3 7. Rb8 7... Rxe7 { Your rook takes on e7 at last, because his own rook left e8 to chase your runner on b3. } 8. Rxb3+ 8... Ka1 { Both pawns are gone and only the rooks are left. Neither king can ever be mated, so the game is drawn. } 0-1


[Event "Rook endgames: a busy rook beats a sleepy one"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "teaching"]
[IntendedResult "draw"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/GCG0qpGy"]
[FEN "8/8/2P5/2R5/kr6/8/6K1/8 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/2P5/2R5/kr6/8/6K1/8_b_-_-_0_1"]

{ You are a pawn down and playing for the draw. A new idea: a busy rook, and yours sits on b4 beside your king doing nothing at all. [%cal Yb4b8] }
1... Rb8 { It leaves the king and takes the only job that matters. [%csl Gb8] } (1... Rb2+ { A check, and then another, and none of them help. } 2. Kf3 Rb3+ 3. Ke4 { The pawn just walks. Lost. }) (1... Rb6 { Attacking the pawn from the side does not stop it. } 2. Rc4+ Kb5 3. c7 { It queens. Lost. }) (1... Rb7 { One row too high, and the pawn eats you. } 2. cxb7 { Lost. Your rook stood right where the pawn wanted to capture. }) (1... Rc4 { You offer the trade, and the pawn wins the race. } 2. Rxc4+ { Lost. Without rooks on the board, the pawn simply runs. }) (1... Kb3 { The king wanders and the rook stays asleep. } 2. Kf3 Rb8 3. Ke4 { Too slow. Lost. }) 2. c7 { The pawn runs for the last row. Get in front of it. [%cal Yb8c8] } 2... Rc8 { The only square in the world that stops it. Frozen for ever. [%csl Gc8][%tail] } (2... Rg8+ { A check first, and it costs you the tempo. } 3. Kf3 Rc8 4. Ke4 { His king arrives in time. Lost. }) (2... Ra8 { The right row, the wrong column. } 3. Rc4+ Ka5 4. Rc5+ { Lost. Your rook sat behind the pawn instead of in front of it, and the checks find your king instead. }) (2... Rb7 { Behind the pawn is too late once it is on the seventh. } 3. Rc4+ { Lost. The check drives your king off and the pawn queens next. }) (2... Kb4 { The king comes, and you leave the rook where the pawn can eat it. } 3. cxb8=Q+ { A new queen, and your rook with it. Lost. }) 3. Rc1 3... Kb4 4. Rb1+ 4... Ka4 { You step aside. His rook can check all day, but yours never moves from c8 and his pawn never moves at all. Drawn. } 0-1


[Event "Rook endgames: check him from far away"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[IntendedResult "draw"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/4sAAyheO"]
[FEN "4k3/1R6/4PK2/8/8/8/8/r7 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/4k3/1R6/4PK2/8/8/8/8/r7_b_-_-_0_1"]

{ Mate is one move away on the back row, and blocking will not save you. Down a pawn, the draw is all you want: find the move. }
1... Rf1+ { Down the f column, five squares from his king, where he can never chase you. [%csl Gf1][%tail] } (1... Ra7 { You guard the back row, and forget the other one. } 2. Rb8# { Mate. Your king is walled in by its own pawns with no square to answer the check. }) (1... Ra6 { The same idea, the same mate. } 2. Rb8# { Mate. The rook delivers it down the open b column. }) (1... Ra8 { Blocking on the back row, and he simply steps aside. } 2. Rg7 Kd8 3. Rg8+ { You are lost. The check drives your king off the row and mate follows. }) (1... Kd8 { The king runs, and the rook comes with him. } 2. Rb8+ Kc7 3. Rf8 { Lost. His rook cuts yours off from the whole board. }) (1... Rd1 { A check would have saved you. This blocks nothing. } 2. Rb8+ Rd8 3. Rxd8+ { Lost. The block cost you the rook as well as the game. }) 2. Kg6 2... Rg1+ 3. Kh6 3... Rh1+ { Check again. His king can run, but it can never trap your rook or free the pawn at the same time. Drawn. } 0-1


[Event "Rook endgames: give the pawn, free the rook"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[IntendedResult "draw"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/eDO2sKMh"]
[FEN "3R4/8/7P/8/3p4/7k/r7/1K6 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/3R4/8/7P/8/3p4/7k/r7/1K6_b_-_-_0_1"]

{ His pawn on h6 is nearly home and his king on b1 is about to eat your rook on a2. Something has to give, so hold the draw. }
1... Ra7 { Up to the seventh! Your pawn falls, but that h pawn can never pass you. [%csl Ga7][%cal Ga7h7][%tail] } (1... d3 { You push your own pawn and lose the rook. } 2. Kxa2 Kg4 3. Rxd3 { Both pawns gone, and lost. }) (1... Ra1+ { A check right next to his king. } 2. Kxa1 { Lost. Your rook walked straight into the king it was checking. }) (1... Ra8 { You offer a trade, and the h pawn queens. } 2. Rxa8 { Lost. His rook takes yours, and the h pawn runs home alone. }) (1... Ra6 { One row short, and he pushes past you. } 2. h7 Rb6+ 3. Kc2 { The pawn queens. Lost. The check costs a tempo, and his pawn runs past on the very next move. }) (1... Rd2 { Guarding your pawn is exactly what you cannot afford. } 2. Kc1 Rf2 3. h7 { Lost. Your rook is stuck watching a pawn that was never the danger. }) 2. h7 2... Rxh7 { Your rook simply takes it. His last pawn is gone, and the draw is safe. } 0-1


[Event "Rook endgames: the Philidor defence"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "teaching"]
[IntendedResult "draw"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/iwpoPqvD"]
[FEN "2k5/6R1/7r/1KP5/8/8/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/2k5/6R1/7r/1KP5/8/8/8/8_w_-_-_0_1"]

{ Meet the Philidor defence, the way a pawn down is held: your king on c8 stands in front of his pawn, and your rook waits on the sixth row. }
1. c6 { He has pushed past your rook. That is your signal: drop right down. [%cal Yh6h1] } 1... Rh1 { Now you check him from the first row for ever, and he can never hide. [%csl Gh1][%cal Gh1h5][%tail] } (1... Rh2 { Also a draw. Any square down there gives the same endless checks. } 2. Rg8+ Kc7 { Still holding: your rook checks from the first row just the same. }) (1... Rh3 { The same, and just as good. } 2. Rg8+ Kc7 { Drawn. Every square on the row gives the same endless checks. }) (1... Rh4 { Also fine. Getting down is what matters, not how far. } 2. Rg8+ Kc7 { Drawn. His king can never escape a rook checking along an empty row. }) (1... Rxc6 { A free pawn, and a lost ending. } 2. Kxc6 Kd8 3. Ra7 { King and rook against a bare king. Lost. }) (1... Rh5+ { A check from too close. His king simply walks at you. } 2. Kb6 Rd5 3. Rg8+ { Lost. Your rook is too close to check from, and the pawn queens next. }) (1... Kd8 { Your king leaves the pawn's square. } 2. Kb6 Ke8 3. Kb7 { The pawn queens. Lost. Nothing was left behind to stop it. }) (1... Rh8 { Dropping back is the idea, but the first row was the target, not the eighth. } 2. Kb6 Kb8 3. Rb7+ { Lost. Your rook sits on the wrong row to give a single useful check. }) (1... Rh7 { You offer the trade and he takes it. } 2. Rxh7 { Lost. The rook that was holding the draw is gone. }) 2. Kb6 2... Rb1+ 3. Kc5 3... Rc1+ { Check again, and the pattern never ends: his king can wander all day, but your rook always finds the row it needs. Drawn. } 0-1


[Event "Rook endgames: your king belongs in front"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[IntendedResult "draw"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/m3sJI5LK"]
[FEN "4k3/8/6P1/6K1/8/8/3R4/r7 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/4k3/8/6P1/6K1/8/8/3R4/r7_b_-_-_0_1"]

{ His pawn on g6 has two steps left and his king on g5 is escorting it. Saving the draw is all you can hope for, so where does your king have to be? }
1... Kf8 { In front of the pawn. That single square is the whole defence. [%csl Gf8] } (1... Rg1+ { A check instead, and his king walks round it. } 2. Kf6 Rf1+ 3. Kg7 { The pawn queens. Lost. The checks never touched the pawn's own road home. }) (1... Ke7 { One square out, and it is already too late. } 2. Kh6 Rh1+ 3. Kg7 { Lost. His king reaches g7 and the pawn queens next move. }) (1... Rf1 { Guarding the queening square from the side is not enough. } 2. Kh6 Rh1+ 3. Kg7 { Lost. The check comes too late to keep his king off g7. }) (1... Ra5+ { A check that pushes him exactly where he wanted to go. } 2. Kh6 Ra6 3. Kh7 { Lost. His king reaches the corner and shields the pawn all the way home. }) (1... Ra7 { The seventh row does not help against a pawn on the sixth. } 2. Kh6 Kf8 3. Rd8+ { Lost. The check drives your king away and the pawn queens behind it. }) 2. g7+ { He pushes with check. Now help yourself. } 2... Kxg7 { The pawn is gone, and a lone rook can never mate you. Drawn. [%csl Gg7] } (2... Kf7 { Also a draw. The pawn cannot run anywhere. } 3. Rd7+ Kg8 { You will eat the pawn next move. Drawn. }) (2... Kg8 { Also fine, and the pawn falls just the same. } 3. Rd8+ Kxg7 { Drawn. The check buys the tempo to scoop up the pawn all the same. }) (2... Ke7 { You step away from the pawn. } 3. Kg6 { His king walks it home. Lost. }) (2... Ke8 { The same mistake. } 3. g8=Q+ { Lost. A new queen is born with check, and your rook cannot hold a whole queen off. }) 0-1


[Event "Rook endgames: drop back far enough"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "review"]
[IntendedResult "draw"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/xQSoo5al"]
[FEN "8/8/8/8/4k3/R2p4/7r/3K4 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/8/8/4k3/R2p4/7r/3K4_w_-_-_0_1"]

{ You are the one defending now. His pawn is close, and your rook is far too near it. }
1. Ra5 { Far enough back to keep checking. Distance is what a rook lives on. [%csl Ga5][%tail] } (1. Ra6 { Also a draw. Further back is just as good. } 1... d2 2. Ra3 { You still hold. }) (1. Ra7 { The same idea again. Any of these squares holds. } 1... d2 2. Ra3 { Drawn. Your rook slides straight back to checking range. }) (1. Ra8 { All the way back, and also fine. } 1... d2 2. Ra3 { Drawn. Even from the far corner, the rook gets back in time. }) (1. Rxd3 { The free pawn, and it costs you the rook. } 1... Kxd3 2. Ke1 Ra2 { You are lost. His king shelters on the back row while your rook stands alone. }) (1. Ra4+ { A check from right beside him. He just steps up. } 1... Ke3 2. Rg4 Rh1+ { Lost. Your rook has no more useful checks, and the pawn queens. }) (1. Ra1 { Too close to the last row. Now his rook comes with check. } 1... Rh1+ 2. Kd2 Rxa1 { Lost. The check wins the tempo to capture your rook outright. }) (1. Rb3 { Sideways, and it does nothing at all. } 1... Ke3 2. Kc1 Rh1+ { Lost. Check, and there is no square left to block it from. }) (1. Kc1 { The king runs and leaves the rook stranded. } 1... Ke3 2. Rb3 Rh1+ { Lost. Check, and your king has nowhere safe left to hide. }) 1... d2 2. Ra3 2... Kd4 3. Ra4+ { Check, from the same safe distance. His king has no shelter from the checks, and his pawn has no escort. Drawn. } 1-0


[Event "Rook endgames: the Lucena bridge"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "teaching"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/DvfV31FU"]
[FEN "8/5P1k/8/r2K4/4R3/8/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/5P1k/8/r2K4/4R3/8/8/8_w_-_-_0_1"]

{ The famous Lucena. He is checking you, so walk your king straight at the checks. [%cal Yd5e6] }
1. Ke6 { Towards the checking rook, never away from it. [%csl Ge6] } (1. Kd6 { Away from the checks, and the pawn is left on its own. } 1... Kg7 2. f8=Q+ Kxf8 { The queen lived one move. Drawn. }) (1. Kc6 { The same problem, one square further off. } 1... Rf5 2. f8=Q Rxf8 { Drawn. His rook stops the new queen the moment she is born. }) (1. Kd4 { Backwards. The pawn cannot wait for you down there. } 1... Rf5 2. f8=Q Rxf8 { Drawn. Your king never reached the pawn, so his rook simply takes the new queen. }) (1. Kc4 { Too far from everything. } 1... Rf5 2. f8=Q Rxf8 { Drawn. His rook was always close enough to collect the new queen. }) 1... Ra6+ { He checks again. Keep walking at him. [%cal Ye6e7] } 2. Ke7 { Two steps up the ladder, and now your pawn is guarded. [%csl Ge7] } (2. Kf5 { Towards his rook, but away from your pawn. } 2... Kg7 3. f8=Q+ Kxf8 { Drawn. His king reaches f8 in time to take the new queen. }) (2. Kd7 { Sideways, and the pawn is on its own again. } 2... Kg7 3. f8=Q+ Kxf8 { Drawn. Unescorted, the new queen lasts one move. }) (2. Kd5 { Back down the ladder, and all the work is undone. } 2... Rf6 3. f8=Q Rxf8 { Drawn. His rook blocks the pawn's road and then eats the new queen. }) (2. Ke5 { Backwards again, and the climb has to start over. } 2... Kg7 3. f8=Q+ Kxf8 { Drawn. His king strolls over and takes the new queen. }) 2... Ra7+ { Another check. Now step across beside the pawn. [%cal Ye7f6] } 3. Kf6 { Beside the pawn, guarding it, and ready to build. [%csl Gf6] } (3. Kf8 { Also winning, but the checks keep coming. } 3... Ra8+ { Still winning, just slower. }) (3. Ke6 { Also winning, though you have gone back a step. } 3... Ra6+ { Still winning, but here he comes again. }) (3. Ke8 { Also winning, but your king is drifting away. } 3... Ra8+ { Still winning, and still drifting. }) (3. Kd6 { Too far from the pawn now. } 3... Kg7 4. f8=Q+ Kxf8 { Drawn. His king gets there first and takes the new queen. }) (3. Kd8 { The same mistake, and further away still. } 3... Kg7 4. f8=Q+ Kxf8 { Drawn. Your king is a whole column from the action, and his simply takes the new queen. }) 3... Ra6+ { The last check. Block it with the rook. [%cal Ye4e6] } 4. Re6 { The bridge! Your rook blocks the check and the pawn goes home. [%csl Ge6][%cal Gf7f8][%tail] } (4. Ke7 { Also winning: you step out instead of blocking. } 4... Ra7+ { Still winning, but the bridge was cleaner. }) (4. Kg5 { Running from the check leaves the pawn alone. } 4... Kg7 5. f8=Q+ Kxf8 { Drawn. Nobody is left to guard the new queen, and his king takes it. }) (4. Ke5 { Backwards, and the whole climb is wasted. } 4... Kg7 5. f8=Q+ Kxf8 { Drawn. His king wanders back over and takes the new queen. }) (4. Kf5 { The same retreat, and the same result. } 4... Kg7 5. f8=Q+ Kxf8 { Drawn. His king is right there to take the new queen. }) 4... Rxe6+ 5. Kxe6 5... Kg7 6. Ke7 6... Kg6 7. f8=Q { A new queen on f8, safely guarded. That is the bridge: your rook shielded the king from every check, and the pawn walked home. } 1-0


